  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA cold front is pushing southward across the forecast area today \nwith showers and a few strong thunderstorms ahead of it. Much of \nthis thunderstorm activity has already exited northern and central \nWest Virginia, southeast Ohio, and northeast Kentucky, but a line of \nstrong to severe thunderstorms will impact portions of southwest \nVirginia and far southern West Virginia through the late morning and \nmidday hours.\n\nThe widespread convection that occurred earlier this morning has \nlargely stabilized the atmosphere, leaving overcast to broken \nstratus in its wake. Latest high resolution model runs are largely \nin agreement today, showing redevelopment of spotty showers and \nthunderstorms late this afternoon, after 20Z. Additional severe \npotential during the late afternoon and evening is questionable, and \nwill likely depend on how much the atmosphere destabilizes later \ntoday, if at all.\n\nMid-level dry air can be seen on GOES water vapor imagery across the \nGreat Lakes, moving north to south. This dry air should approach the \nforecast area Sunday, bringing drier conditions through at least \nWednesday with high pressure in control.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nMuch more comfortable air for early September will return Sunday in \nthe wake of a cold front. Expect temperatures in the lower to middle \n80s for daytime highs and dew points in the 60s. The comfortable, \nless humid air should stick around through at least Tuesday. The 500-\nmb ridge axis will shift eastward again Tuesday into Wednesday, \nbringing the return of heat and humidity for the second half of the \nwork week. Models show the potential for a few mid-level \ndisturbances to cross Thursday and Friday, which may signal the \nreturn of more showers and storms.",
